  mcq: [
    { id: "mcq-5.2-1", question: "Which item is an expense in the statement of profit or loss of a partnership rather than an appropriation?", choices: ["Interest on a loan from a partner", "Interest on capital", "Partner's salary", "Share of residual profit"], explanation: "A loan from a partner is borrowing, so its interest is a cost of the business and is deducted before profit for the year. Interest on capital, salaries and profit shares are all appropriations." },
    { id: "mcq-5.2-2", question: "Profit for the year is $30,000. Interest on capital totals $4,000, partners' salaries total $10,000 and interest on drawings totals $1,000. What is the residual profit?", choices: ["$17,000", "$15,000", "$16,000", "$45,000"], explanation: "30,000 + 1,000 − 4,000 − 10,000 = $17,000. Interest on drawings is added, not deducted. $15,000 deducts it; $16,000 ignores it; $45,000 adds everything." },
    { id: "mcq-5.2-3", question: "A partner's current account has a debit balance at the year end. What does this mean?", choices: ["The partner has drawn more than has been credited to the account", "The partner has lent money to the business", "The partner has introduced additional capital", "The business owes the partner money"], explanation: "Credits to a current account are the partner's earnings; debits are drawings. A debit balance means drawings exceed earnings, so the partner owes the business." },
    { id: "mcq-5.2-4", question: "Partners A and B share profits 3:1. The residual profit is $26,000. What is B's share?", choices: ["$6,500", "$19,500", "$8,667", "$13,000"], explanation: "The ratio 3:1 has four parts, so B receives 1/4 × $26,000 = $6,500. $19,500 is A's share; $8,667 uses thirds; $13,000 assumes equal shares." },
    { id: "mcq-5.2-5", question: "Which entry records a partner's salary in a partnership with fixed capital accounts?", choices: ["Debit appropriation account; credit partner's current account", "Debit statement of profit or loss; credit bank", "Debit partner's capital account; credit appropriation account", "Debit partner's current account; credit appropriation account"], explanation: "The salary is an appropriation of profit credited to the partner in the current account. It does not pass through the capital account and is not a business expense." },
    { id: "mcq-5.2-6", question: "Why does a partnership agreement often charge interest on drawings?", choices: ["To discourage partners from withdrawing cash early in the year", "To reward partners for investing capital", "To compensate a partner who works longer hours", "To pay partners for lending money to the business"], explanation: "Interest on drawings is a charge against the partner. It makes drawings more expensive and so protects the cash the business needs. Interest on capital rewards investment; a salary rewards work; loan interest rewards lending." },
    { id: "mcq-5.2-7", question: "Where is a loan from a partner, repayable in five years, shown in the statement of financial position?", choices: ["Non-current liabilities", "Capital account of the partner", "Current account of the partner", "Current liabilities"], explanation: "The loan is money the partnership owes and is not due within one year, so it is a non-current liability. It is separate from the partner's capital and current accounts." },
    { id: "mcq-5.2-8", question: "Profit for the year is $8,000. Interest on capital totals $3,000 and a partner's salary is $7,000. Profits are shared equally. What is each partner's share of the residual?", choices: ["$1,000 loss", "$1,000 profit", "$4,000 profit", "$2,000 loss"], explanation: "8,000 − 3,000 − 7,000 = a residual loss of $2,000, shared equally as $1,000 loss each. The appropriations are not cancelled because profit is small." },
    { id: "mcq-5.2-9", question: "Which of the following is a disadvantage of a partnership compared with a sole trader?", choices: ["Decisions may take longer because partners must agree", "Less capital is available", "The workload cannot be shared", "Losses are borne by one person only"], explanation: "Shared decision making can slow the business and cause disagreements. The other options describe disadvantages of a sole trader, not of a partnership." },
    { id: "mcq-5.2-10", question: "A partner's current account shows: balance b/d $900 credit, interest on capital $1,500, share of profit $11,000, drawings $12,800, interest on drawings $300. What is the closing balance?", choices: ["$300 credit", "$600 credit", "$1,500 debit", "$300 debit"], explanation: "Credits 900 + 1,500 + 11,000 = 13,400. Debits 12,800 + 300 = 13,100. Closing balance $300 credit. Omitting interest on drawings gives $600 credit; putting the opening balance on the debit side gives $1,500 debit." },
  ],
