  mcq: [
    { id: "mcq-5.5-1", question: "Which item appears in a receipts and payments account but not in an income and expenditure account?", choices: ["Purchase of new equipment", "Depreciation of equipment", "Subscriptions in arrears at the year end", "Rent owing at the year end"], explanation: "Buying equipment is a cash payment and capital expenditure, so it appears in receipts and payments only. Depreciation, arrears and accruals are adjustments that appear in the income and expenditure account." },
    { id: "mcq-5.5-2", question: "Subscriptions received during the year were $5,400. Arrears were $300 at the start and $500 at the end. Advances were $200 at the start and $150 at the end. What is the subscriptions income?", choices: ["$5,650", "$5,150", "$5,550", "$5,400"], explanation: "5,400 + 500 − 300 + 200 − 150 = $5,650. $5,150 reverses all the adjustments; $5,550 adjusts for the advances in the wrong direction; $5,400 makes no adjustment at all." },
    { id: "mcq-5.5-3", question: "How are subscriptions in advance at the year end shown in a club's statement of financial position?", choices: ["Current liability", "Current asset", "Added to the accumulated fund", "Non-current liability"], explanation: "The club has received money for a service it has not yet provided, so it owes next year's membership. That is a short-term liability." },
    { id: "mcq-5.5-4", question: "What is the club equivalent of a sole trader's capital?", choices: ["Accumulated fund", "Surplus", "Subscriptions", "Receipts and payments balance"], explanation: "The accumulated fund is total assets less total liabilities, built up from past surpluses, just as capital is built from profits retained." },
    { id: "mcq-5.5-5", question: "A club cafe had sales $4,000, opening inventory $200, purchases $2,500, closing inventory $300 and cafe wages $600. What amount is transferred to the income and expenditure account?", choices: ["$1,000 profit", "$1,600 profit", "$1,500 profit", "$4,000 income"], explanation: "Cost of sales = 200 + 2,500 − 300 = 2,400. Gross profit = 1,600. Less wages 600 = $1,000 profit transferred. Only the net profit moves to the income and expenditure account." },
    { id: "mcq-5.5-6", question: "A club's assets at the start of the year were $14,000 and its liabilities were $2,500. During the year it made a surplus of $1,800. What was the accumulated fund at the end of the year?", choices: ["$13,300", "$11,500", "$15,800", "$9,700"], explanation: "Opening fund = 14,000 − 2,500 = 11,500. Add surplus 1,800 = $13,300. $11,500 is the opening fund; $15,800 forgets the liabilities." },
    { id: "mcq-5.5-7", question: "Which of the following is a source of income in an income and expenditure account?", choices: ["Profit on an annual dinner", "A loan received from the bank", "Sale proceeds of old equipment", "Subscriptions received in advance for next year"], explanation: "The profit on an event is income earned this year. A loan is a liability, sale proceeds reduce an asset, and advance subscriptions belong to next year." },
    { id: "mcq-5.5-8", question: "At the year end a club has rent owing of $400 and insurance prepaid of $150. How are these shown in the statement of financial position?", choices: ["Rent as a current liability; insurance as a current asset", "Both as current liabilities", "Rent as a current asset; insurance as a current liability", "Both deducted from the accumulated fund"], explanation: "Rent owing is an accrual, an amount the club must pay, so it is a liability. Prepaid insurance is a benefit still to be received, so it is an asset." },
    { id: "mcq-5.5-9", question: "Subscriptions of $100 owed by a member who has left the club are written off. What is the effect?", choices: ["Expenditure increases by $100 and subscriptions in arrears decrease by $100", "Income increases by $100", "Subscriptions in advance decrease by $100", "The accumulated fund increases by $100"], explanation: "The write-off removes an asset that will not be collected and is charged as an expense in the income and expenditure account, reducing the surplus." },
    { id: "mcq-5.5-10", question: "Why does a club prepare an income and expenditure account rather than relying only on a receipts and payments account?", choices: ["To show whether income earned covered expenditure incurred in the year", "To show how much cash is in the bank", "To calculate the subscriptions received", "To list the capital items purchased"], explanation: "The income and expenditure account applies the matching principle to measure the surplus or deficit for the period. The receipts and payments account already shows the cash position." },
  ],
