      body: "A full incomplete records question gives a list of opening balances, a summary of the bank account, a list of closing balances and a few notes. The order of work is always the same. First, prepare an opening statement of affairs to find opening capital. Second, use total receivables and payables accounts to find credit sales and purchases, and add any cash sales and cash purchases. Third, use a cash summary if a cash figure is missing. Fourth, prepare the statement of profit or loss using these sales and purchases, the inventories given, and expenses adjusted for accruals and prepayments and depreciation. Fifth, prepare the statement of financial position, taking the closing figures from the question.\n\nFinally, check. Opening capital plus profit less drawings plus capital introduced should equal the closing capital shown by net assets. If it does, every reconstructed figure fits together. If it does not, the most common causes are a missing cash drawing, discount allowed or received left out of the control account, or an accrual used in the statement of profit or loss but omitted from the statement of financial position.\n\nIt is a long question, but every step uses a skill from earlier topics. Keep the workings labelled and in order, and the marks follow.",
